21:45:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It makes sense that it feels like “just how men are” when the men closest to you have repeatedly had short fuses. That pattern can be exhausting, and it can make you feel like you always have to manage someone else’s anger. But their anger is still **their behavior and responsibility**, not an unavoidable fact of being male. Some men do grow up being taught that anger, control, or intimidation are acceptable ways to handle frustration—but many don’t act that way, and they can choose to work on it. For your cousin, the practical point is: she doesn’t need to debate whether he “means it” or whether it’s normal. She can respond to the impact: - “I won’t stay in a conversation where I’m being yelled at.” - “I’ll talk when you can speak respectfully.” - Leave, hang up, or get support rather than trying to calm him down. - Avoid being alone with him if his temper includes threats, blocking exits, breaking things, or physical aggression. - Tell someone trustworthy what is happening; don’t keep it private to protect him. And for you: it may be worth recognizing that repeated exposure to short-tempered men can make anger feel inevitable in relationships. It isn’t. A counselor or support service can help you sort through those experiences and strengthen boundaries, especially if any of the behavior was abusive or frightening.
