21:45:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Testosterone can be one influence on behavior, but it is not a switch that makes men inevitably aggressive or abusive. On average, men have higher testosterone and may show somewhat more **physical aggression** at a population level. But the overlap between individuals is huge, and real-world anger or cruelty is shaped heavily by things like personality, stress, alcohol/drugs, learned family behavior, social norms, trauma, and whether someone has learned emotional regulation. Testosterone is also linked to seeking status or reacting to threats to status—which can show up as competitiveness, protectiveness, confidence, generosity, or aggression depending on the person and setting. Most importantly: biology may help explain a tendency in some circumstances; it does not excuse yelling, intimidation, controlling behavior, or violence. Men can learn to pause, leave a heated conversation, seek therapy, avoid substances that worsen anger, and take responsibility for how they treat people. So your cousin doesn’t need to assume her brother is incapable of behaving differently. She can set boundaries based on what he actually does, and if his behavior is frightening or unsafe, prioritize distance and outside support rather than trying to manage his temper for him.
