21:45:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Evolutionary psychology can offer hypotheses about why aggression may have been advantageous in some ancestral situations—such as competition, defense, or status conflicts. And, on average, men are more represented in some forms of physical aggression and violence. But “men evolved with aggressive capacities” is very different from “men are hardwired to be brutish and can’t control themselves.” A few important distinctions: - **Group averages don’t define every individual.** There is enormous variation among men, and many men are calm, conflict-avoidant, and emotionally regulated. - **Aggression is not one fixed trait.** Physical aggression, irritability, dominance, competitiveness, assertiveness, and violence are related but not interchangeable. - **Humans are also evolved for cooperation and care.** Bonding, empathy, parenting, reciprocity, and conflict repair were adaptive too. - **Environment and choices matter.** Social expectations, upbringing, stress, substance use, peer groups, and learned coping skills strongly affect whether someone acts aggressively. - **An explanation is not an excuse.** Even if a tendency had biological roots, people remain responsible for not yelling, threatening, controlling, or hurting others. So it is reasonable for your cousin to be alert to her brother’s actual temper and to protect herself accordingly. But treating aggression as inevitable in men can lead her to normalize harmful treatment or feel she has to tolerate it. She doesn’t. The standard should be simple: respectful behavior, accountability, and safety—regardless of gender.
