21:45:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It isn’t controversial to say that, on average, boys and men may be more physically rough, more competitive, or more involved in violent behavior than girls and women. Those are real population-level patterns. What is worth pushing back on is the leap from a broad average or cliché to “men are inherently brutes” or “they can’t help having a short fuse.” “Boys will be boys” has often been used to minimize bullying, harassment, rage, or violence that should be addressed. A more accurate version is: - Some sex-linked biological differences may affect average tendencies. - Culture and upbringing also strongly shape how people express anger and conflict. - Individual differences are large. - Every person is still responsible for managing their behavior. So if her brother is rough but respectful and responsive when told to stop, clear boundaries may be enough. If he is yelling, intimidating, threatening, or violent, that is not something she should write off as normal male behavior. She should seek support and prioritize safety.
