21:45:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Many animal species do show more male–male competition, especially where males compete for mates, territory, or rank. But it is not true that males are *always* the aggressive or dominant sex: in some species females are more dominant, females compete intensely, or both sexes are aggressive in different contexts. Humans also aren’t reducible to one animal pattern. We have biological influences, but we also have language, norms, moral judgment, long-term relationships, institutions, and the ability to learn self-regulation. Men may have, on average, greater physical strength and higher rates of certain kinds of physical aggression, but that does not mean a particular man is destined to be hostile—or that he is unable to control it. The useful distinction is: - **“Some average sex differences exist”**: reasonable to discuss. - **“Men are naturally brutish, so aggression is inevitable or excusable”**: not accurate or helpful. For your cousin, she should take her brother’s actual behavior seriously without treating it as unavoidable “male nature.” If he is merely boisterous, she can set limits. If he scares, threatens, or harms her, she should involve trusted family, local support services, or emergency help as appropriate.
