21:49:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re not dumb, and you’re not being told that your experiences were imaginary. If the men around you—your dad, brothers, exes—were volatile, intimidating, controlling, or unsafe, then you learned something real: their behavior could hurt you, and you needed to stay alert. Family warnings can come from real experience too. It makes sense that repeated exposure would make the pattern feel broad and unquestionable. The distinction isn’t “you saw nothing” versus “you saw everything wrong.” It’s: - Your experience may show that **some men in your life were aggressive or unsafe**. - Broader evidence supports that men, on average, have higher rates of certain forms of physical aggression and violence. - Neither point requires you to assume every man will behave that way—or to accept it from anyone who does. You do not need to convince yourself that men are harmless, and you do not need to lower your guard to be fair. You can trust your judgment about danger, set firm boundaries, leave situations that feel wrong, and seek support when someone’s conduct is threatening. The important thing is that aggression is taken seriously and never treated as something you have to endure.
