21:50:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Testosterone and physical strength are part of the picture, but they are not the whole cause. - **Physical strength** can increase someone’s capacity to cause harm and can affect power dynamics. - **Testosterone** is associated with some forms of dominance-seeking, competitiveness, and aggression in certain contexts, but it does not reliably produce violence by itself. - **Upbringing and social conditions** are not “noise”: exposure to violence, norms about masculinity, alcohol or drug use, stress, peer reinforcement, access to weapons, and consequences for abuse all strongly affect whether aggressive behavior occurs. A simple reality check is that men with similar biology can behave very differently across families, cultures, and situations. Rates and forms of violence change over time and vary widely between societies—far more than human testosterone levels do. That indicates social context has a major causal role, alongside biology. So it is reasonable to say male biology contributes to average differences in physical aggression. It is not accurate to treat it as the single real explanation, or as proof that men cannot choose nonviolent behavior.
