21:54:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You do not need to “let your guard down” to avoid making a blanket assumption about every man. A safer middle position is **cautious, evidence-based trust**: - Let trust build gradually from consistent respectful behavior. - Keep practical safety habits with new people: meet publicly, tell someone your plans, maintain your own transport/phone/money, and leave if you feel uneasy. - Treat specific warning signs seriously: anger at “no,” jealousy, controlling behavior, pressure, threats, humiliation, isolating you, violence toward anyone, or blaming others for their temper. - Don’t rely on gender alone to override what you observe—either positively (“he’s a nice guy, so it’s fine”) or negatively (“he’s male, so he must be dangerous”). Assuming the worst about every man may feel protective, especially after painful experiences, but it can keep you in constant fear and is less useful than learning to recognize behavior and maintain boundaries. You can be alert without granting automatic trust. And if someone makes you feel unsafe, you do not need certainty, proof, or agreement from others to create distance. If there is immediate danger, contact emergency services or a local domestic-violence support service.
