21:54:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That makes sense emotionally. If the problem is “men are just like this,” then it can feel less painful than wondering whether you missed signs, trusted too soon, or somehow caused it. But someone else’s anger, coercion, or abuse is **not your failure to predict perfectly**. People who behave badly often do not show it clearly at first; they may be charming, apologetic, or escalate gradually. No one can screen perfectly, and responsibility always belongs to the person choosing to intimidate, control, or hurt someone. You can let go of self-blame without needing to conclude that all men are inevitably dangerous: - You were entitled to expect basic respect. - Trusting someone who later mistreated you was not consent to that mistreatment. - You can learn warning signs and use safety boundaries, but they are tools—not a burden to prevent another person’s choices. - If repeated experiences have left you feeling constantly on guard, talking with a trauma-informed counselor or abuse-support service could help. That is support, not an admission that you did anything wrong. The most protective belief may be: **I don’t have to identify every risk perfectly; I can take discomfort seriously, leave when I need to, and I am never responsible for someone else’s aggression.**
